// @test: run
// @level: L1
// @tier: 0
// @tags: protocols, defaults, stdlib
// @timeout: 30000
// @skip: T0952 is open — a protocol default body does not survive the bake,
//        so this file does not merely fail, it PANICS at run time with
//        "method 'Counter.fold' not found". The skip comes off in the commit
//        that makes stdlib protocol defaults inheritable, not by editing the
//        expectation. Do not "fix" this file by writing `fold` on Counter:
//        that is the workaround every core/ type already applies (23 explicit
//        `fn fold` across the tree), and it is why nothing inside the stdlib
//        ever noticed.
// @expected-stdout-begin
// fold 3
// count 3
// @expected-stdout-end
// @description: A type implementing a stdlib protocol inherits that
//   protocol's default methods.
//
//   `core/base/iterator.vr:378` gives `Iterator.fold` a body, so a type that
//   supplies `next` gets `fold` for free. That is the whole point of a
//   default: 124 methods in `core/` carry one.
//
//   It works for a protocol declared in the SAME compilation and not for one
//   loaded from the archive, which locates the defect precisely — default
//   bodies survive within a compilation and are lost through the bake.
//   `VbcCodegen` already monomorphises defaults onto every implementor via
//   `ProtocolInfo.default_methods`, but that registry is filled from source
//   declarations, so an archive-loaded protocol contributes nothing and there
//   is no dispatch-time fallback either — the body is not in the archive
//   under any name. The candidate list in the panic shows only concrete
//   types' `fold`.
//
//   The sibling `implement_block_completeness_leaves_these_alone.vr` covers
//   the same feature for a LOCALLY declared protocol, and passes. Reading the
//   two together is what shows this is a bake boundary and not a protocol
//   feature that was never implemented.

mount core.prelude.*;

type Counter is { n: Int };

implement Iterator for Counter {
    type Item = Int;

    fn next(&mut self) -> Maybe<Int> {
        if self.n <= 0 {
            Maybe.None
        } else {
            self.n = self.n - 1;
            Maybe.Some(self.n)
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut a = Counter { n: 3 };
    let total: Int = a.fold(0, |acc, x| acc + x);
    print(f"fold {total}");

    let mut b = Counter { n: 3 };
    let n: Int = b.count();
    print(f"count {n}");
}
